 ],
 "literal": "Even if the useless town sleeps, we do not sleep indeed, / above the hedge adjacent to our house, / the nocci tree with dark clusters and leaves like a peacock's foot, / on its beautiful, abundant, soft branches, / having heard clearly indeed the falling of its sapphire-resembling flowers.",
 "translation": "Even if this entire town falls asleep, we do not sleep. For, from the nocci tree with dark clusters and leaves shaped like a peacock's foot, growing above the fence adjacent to our house, we clearly hear the sound of sapphire-like flowers falling from its beautiful, soft branches.",
 "modern": "இந்த ஊரே தூங்கினாலும், நாம் தூங்குவதில்லை. நம் வீட்டிற்குப் பக்கத்தில் உள்ள வேலிக்கு மேலே, மயிலின் அடி போன்ற இலைகளையும் கரிய பூங்கொத்துகளையும் கொண்ட நொச்சி மரத்தின் அழகிய மெல்லிய கிளைகளிலிருந்து, நீலமணி போன்ற பூக்கள் உதிரும் சத்தத்தை மிகத் தெளிவாகக் கேட்டுக்கொண்டே இருக்கிறோம்.",
 "explanation": "The companion speaks to the heroine (or to herself within earshot of the hero) during the night, highlighting the heroine's sleeplessness and pining. Even if the entire town is asleep, they cannot sleep because they are constantly listening to the soft sound of the sapphire-like flowers of the backyard nocci tree falling onto the ground. The nocci tree, with its peacock-foot-like leaves and dark clusters, stands right by the fence. The delicate sound of its falling flowers, which would normally go unnoticed, is so loud to their sleepless, hyper-alert ears, emphasizing the quietness of the night and the depth of their distress. This belongs to the Marutam landscape, where domestic spaces and subtle nature reflect the emotional tension of waiting and longing.",
